For our game, we need more than the names of the generals. We need every brigade, its constituent regiments, cavalry, artillery, and the chain of command that existed when it fought.

There's an important complication: no single order of battle covers the entire campaign accurately. Jackson began with a few thousand men in March, absorbed Edward Johnson's command in May, and joined forces with Richard Ewell's division. Meanwhile, the Union forces under Banks, Frémont, and Shields operated as separate commands whose organizations and attachments changed substantially.

The National Park Service estimates that Jackson had approximately 3,500–3,800 men at Kernstown and around 16,000–17,000 by late May. The combined Union forces opposing him across the wider campaign were much larger, but they were not concentrated in one army.&#x20;

