## Part VI — How I would represent this in Hex of Steel

For the campaign, each historical brigade becomes an independently movable counter, with attached or separately represented artillery and cavalry depending on their operational role.

The headquarters system should reflect actual military relationships on the chosen date. A Confederate brigade assigned to Ewell should not be treated as a brigade reporting directly to Winder, nor should Frémont automatically control troops attached to Banks simply because both are Union commanders.

A few particularly important mechanics follow from the historical roster:

| Historical circumstance                               | Required game treatment                                         |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Johnson's independent command joins Jackson           | Transfer formations without duplicating regiments               |
| Garnett is removed from command                       | Replace his commander record, retaining the brigade's identity  |
| Ewell joins Jackson                                   | Introduce a new major headquarters and its subordinate brigades |
| Banks loses Shields' division                         | Remove that formation from Banks' command hierarchy             |
| Front Royal garrison is isolated                      | Preserve the garrison as a distinct command                     |
| Ashby is killed                                       | Trigger command succession and possible disruption              |
| Elzey and Steuart are wounded                         | Activate their successors without destroying the brigades       |
| Frémont and Shields approach Port Republic separately | Keep independent headquarters, command radii, and movement      |

These are valuable examples of why a persistent unit roster matters more than simply producing six unrelated battle files.

### Historical strength estimates

We should not assign every brigade an invented strength of 2,000 or 3,000 soldiers. The regimental lists above establish organization, but they don't establish accurate effective strength on every date.

For a preliminary campaign balance, these are useful NPS estimates of forces associated with individual battles:

| Engagement       | Union    | Confederate                              |
| ---------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| First Kernstown  | \~8,500  | \~3,800                                  |
| McDowell         | \~6,500  | Figures vary by engaged/available troops |
| Front Royal      | \~1,063  | \~3,000 actively engaged                 |
| First Winchester | \~6,500  | \~16,000                                 |
| Cross Keys       | \~11,500 | \~5,800                                  |
| Port Republic    | \~3,500  | \~6,000                                  |

Approximate battle-level figures, not a single simultaneous campaign strength return. The McDowell comparison is especially sensitive to whether troops present or directly engaged are counted.

